  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Which of the following relations are antisymmetric? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $5∣xy,$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x-y<8,$ defined on $N$\n\n- $X\\,T\\,Y$ if and only if $X⊆Y,$ defined on $P(R)$",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not antisymmetric. Notice that \n\n- $(1,5)\\in R$ since $5∣(1\\cdot 5),$ and\n\n- $(5,1)\\in R$ since $5∣(5\\cdot 1).$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not antisymmetric. Notice that \n\n- $(1,3)\\in S$ since $3-1<8,$ and\n\n- $(3,1)\\in S$ since $1-3<8.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is antisymmetric. Indeed, if $(X,Y)\\in T$ and $(Y,X)\\in T,$ then $X=Y.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nX⊆Y\\,\\text{and}\\,Y⊆X\\,⟹\\,X=Y\n$$\n\nfor all $X,Y\\in P(R).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\"",
